  "posture_summary": "Lithuania enters the week of the May 1 LSDP congress in a state of compounding domestic crisis alongside robust concrete security infrastructure advancement. The security dimension has advanced materially: the Seimas voted 105-12-4 to establish permanent training infrastructure near the Suwałki Gap (Kapčiamiestis), expedited drone interceptor procurement was authorized, and the US approved a $214M missile sale — concrete actions that move the 2030 national division goal forward. The alignment dimension is confirmed and actively shaped: President Nausėda at the Cyprus EU summit asserted Lithuanian positions on NATO primacy, Ukraine accession, and eastern flank budget with multiple sources confirming the content independently. The domestic dimension, however, has intensified on four simultaneous fronts: the LRT law controversy produced Lithuania's largest civil society mobilization this wave (30,000 protesters), forcing a pre-congress rhetorical retreat from the PM; a catastrophic televised interview solidified a PM competence narrative across stakeholder groups; the Paluckas illicit enrichment prosecution is the second consecutive LSDP-associated former PM facing charges in two weeks; and the governing coalition is polling at 48.2% — barely above the majority threshold — while LSDP loses members and PPNA gains them. Democratic architecture remains intact: anti-corruption institutions are functioning, opposition is working through constitutional channels, and civil society is mobilizing effectively. The May 1 LSDP congress is now the single most consequential near-term event, determining both party leadership and the coalition's forward trajectory. PPNA's unexpected organizational growth (+190 members) during its period of maximum controversy revises downward the confidence in the 'rapid fragmentation' prediction from the populist cycle analysis.",
